+I developed the tools in this chapter to couple neural controllers to Gait2392, a human musculoskeletal model, and to mechanical models based on the computer-aided design (CAD) of our bipedal robot. Slow simulation and hand tuning in an earlier course project with Connor Morrow made it difficult to repeat the same experiment after changing the controller or the mechanical model. A scripted version of the walker in the MuJoCo physics engine made automatic tuning possible. The subsequent experiments use the human model, a robot counterpart with relocated muscle paths, and a model built from the lab robot's own geometry. I built a graphical editor to construct neural circuits for these experiments. A simulated knee reflex tests whether a sensory signal passes through a motor neuron and muscle to produce joint motion. The final experiments use scripts to tune controllers, test whether the feet alternate under load, and measure how much a faster neural calculation saves over a complete trial.
 
 \section{The Limits of Hand Tuning in AnimatLab}\label{sec:prior_walker}\label{sec:animatlab_results}\label{sec:sns_gaps}
 
-I built my first simulated walker in AnimatLab, a graphical program that couples a neural circuit to a moving body \citep{cofer_animatlab_2010}. To tune the walker, myself and my colleague, Morrow, changed currents, synaptic weights, and muscle parameters by hand and ran it again. Each motor neuron recruits a muscle, the muscle pulls on the limb, and sensory signals from the moving limb return to the circuit. A change in any part of that loop can alter the next step. Runs were slow, making hand tuning impractical for a large parameter search. I wanted to change the controller or the leg geometry, repeat the same test, and identify where the response changed.
