+I built my first neuromechanical model in AnimatLab, a graphical program in which a neural circuit can activate muscles and receive sensory signals from the moving body \citep{cofer_animatlab_2010}. For the course project, Morrow and I connected the circuit of \citet{deng_neuromechanical_2019} to the sagittal-plane biped of \citet{li_bipedal_2017} and adjusted neural currents, synaptic weights, and muscle parameters by hand. The circuit supplied a common rhythm to both legs, and opposing muscles pulled on each limb. I added a passive toe joint. The legs cycled in suspension, but the model tipped when released toward the ground (Figure~\ref{fig:animatlab_model_architecture}). Hunt and I reported the suspended model in a poster for the NIH BRAIN Initiative Investigators Meeting \citep{bolenTorqueValuesArtificialKnee2021June15-17}. Each parameter change required another slow run, making a systematic search impractical.
 
