 \section{Conclusion}
 
-Restoring the Gait2392 muscle-force scales gave me a human reference for recruitment tests. The BPA model predicts tension from pressure and routed length. In the same \qty{16}{\second} Gait2392 trial, sparse neural execution cut the wall time for the full process from \qty{199.624}{\second} to \qty{29.284}{\second}. A future researcher can edit a pathway or muscle route, fit the controller, and repeat the same test to measure the change in force and motion.
+I developed the SNS-Simscape block library and graphical editor so that a researcher can draw a nonspiking circuit, inspect an individual connection, and generate a Simulink model from the saved circuit. The actuator and sensory blocks link muscle recruitment to force and joint motion in the single-joint demonstrations. For experiments with MuJoCo mechanics, the adapted interface sends actuator commands to a prepared model and returns joint and actuator measurements. The mechanical preparation tools make the Gait2392 human model, its rerouted robot counterpart, and a model assembled from the lab robot's CAD available for controller experiments.
+
+The calibration and recording scripts make comparisons repeatable. A future researcher can load a saved circuit, change one sensory pathway, and repeat the trial with the body, drive, and support held fixed. The recordings show how that change affects neural activity, muscle force, joint motion, and foot contact. A change in muscle routing can be tested with the controller settings held fixed. The sparse neural implementation shortens repeated trials on the tested Gait2392 controller (Table~\ref{tab:sns_runtime}). These capabilities allow researchers to use human mechanics as a reference and simulate how a change in the controller or the robot's design affects coordination.
 
