 I timed one complete Gait2392 process per backend for the 1,025-cell source configuration selected by \texttt{hunt}. Its \qty{16}{\second} stage-1 protocol included \qty{0.6}{\second} of held-pose preparation and \qty{15.4}{\second} on the physics clock. Elapsed process time was \qty{199.624}{\second} with native SNS and \qty{29.284}{\second} with the sparse backend, a 6.82-fold speedup. Within those processes, accumulated neural-call time was \qty{179.079}{\second} and \qty{9.780}{\second}, an 18.31-fold speedup. All 45 captured numerical arrays and the compiled-model hash agreed.
 
 I next compared compact and sparse implementations of the 1,033-cell Hunt-PT+RC circuit. After a separate 1,200-step state-agreement check in each family, the median of three 3,000-update warm neural-kernel timings was \qty{0.8953}{\second} for sparse and \qty{0.5551}{\second} for compact, a factor of 1.613. In one paired \qty{16}{\second} stage-4 diagnostic, evaluator time fell from \qty{92.944}{\second} to \qty{91.516}{\second}, a factor of 1.016. That interval included simulation, capture, and assessment, excluding imports, pre-run setup, and final serialization. All 62 numerical arrays and compiled-model hashes agreed. The backend preserves summation order. Spiking and electrical channels continue to use their existing implementations.
 
 In the bilateral-RG W2L circuit with Ib input ports, the ratio was 1.425 for one warm block of 10,000 neural updates per backend following 5,000 state-comparison steps. The paired \qty{30}{\second} body trajectories agreed exactly. This measurement times the isolated W2L neural updates. Only the first Gait2392 comparison above timed the complete process externally.
 
